## Quick Start

The following shows an extract from an example project and illustrates how to
write plugins for @CMAKE_PROJECT_NAME@.

PROTIP: The source code here contains hyperlinks. Use it to jump to the
documentation of a component.

### Exposing Commands to the Framework

<!-- TODO Note: some of the text here is copy-pasted in dls.2.in -->

Commands are managed by `dls::CommandManager`. You can dynamically add commands
to the `CommandManager` and it will take care of registering the commands
dynamically with the rest of the framework. When the manager goes out of scope,
it removes all of its commands from the framework. Consider the following simple
example:

```cpp
#include <dls2/command/command.hpp>
#include <iostream>

void foo();
double bar(int);

int main()
{
	using dls::ARGVOID;

	dls::CommandManager command_manager;
	command_manager.addCommand<void, ARGVOID>
	(
		// Owner of the command as seen by the framework
		"example_command_project",
		// Name of the command as seen by the framework
		"foo",
		// Documention of the command
		"prints foo"
		// Bind the command into the manager
		std::function<void(ARGVOID)>
		(
			[&](ARGVOID)
			{
				foo();
			}
		)
	);

	command_manager.addCommand<double, int>
	(
		"example_command_project",
		"bar"
		"does nothing useful",
		std::function<double(int)>
		(
			[&](int)
			{
				return bar();
			}
		)
	);
}

void foo()
{
	std::cout << "foo" << std::endl;
}

double bar(int i)
{
	std::cout << "bar: " << i << std::endl;
	return 20.0;
}
```

`dls::ARGVOID` is only required in cases where the command takes no arguments.
This is due to a limitation where C++ deviates from C.

### Logging

<!-- TODO NOTE, some of the text here is copy-pasted in dls.2.in -->
Logging in the framework is achieved through the usage of predetermined log
streams. There are streams available for debugging, system logs, user
communication, error messages and fatal error messages.  These classes have the
same interface as `std::cout`, with the difference that they do not print to
stdout, but instead automatically publish their messages to the framework. They
publish as soon as they are passed `std::endl`.

The classes for logging are:

```cpp
@LOG_TYPES_INCL@
```

Example usage:

```cpp
@LOG_EXAMPLE_CPP_INCL@
```

The user may then run the log layer:

```txt
$ dls -llog
```

and filter out interesting logs using tools such as grep and awk

